# Untitled

## Pluripotent stem cells do not completely maintain normal human steady-state haemopoiesis
P. Dörmer* and G. Ucci†
(Received 11 July 1983; revision accepted 9 November 1983)
Abstract. Since the classical work on the regulation of canine erythropoiesis by Alpen & Cranmore (1959), it has been generally accepted that recognizable bone-marrow cells are continuously replaced from sources of unrecognizable precursors. Although many features of pluripotent stem cells (PSC) and committed haemopoietic precursors have been determined, direct demonstration of a continuous influx, under normal steady-state conditions, from PSC into the recognizable bone-marrow cell compartments is still lacking. There is abundant evidence that PSC, in a number of species, including primates, resemble atypical or immature ("transitional") lymphocytes. By utilizing the technique of quantitative \(^{14}\mathrm{C}\)-autoradiography, we have measured the activities of DNA and protein synthesis in individual bone-marrow cells of two healthy humans. A positive relationship was established between the protein synthesis rate and rate of movement through the cell cycle in all proliferative compartments. Lymphoid cells, considered to contain the fraction of PSC, were found in the lower range of this relationship. These low metabolic rates exclude fast growth as well as short cell-cycle times. In view of the low frequency of the potential PSC in the human bone marrow, amounting to less than \(2\%\), these cells cannot be considered to represent a source continuously supplying the pool of rapidly proliferating, recognizable blast cells in the bone marrow under steady-state conditions. Some self-maintenance of a subcompartment within the pool of recognizable normal bone-marrow blast cells is therefore suggested.
Extended estimates of the number and kinetics of human haemopoietic stem and precursor cells required to maintain a continuous flux of cells through all haemopoietic cell compartments have rendered the question of a continuous supply from the pluripotent stem-cell (PSC) level more enigmatic than clear (Cronkite & Feinendegen, 1976). General agreement exists on the notion that PSC are small cells, slightly larger than small lymphocytes, and that they morphologically resemble the lymphoid series rather than the recognizable bone-marrow blast cells of the red- and white-cell series (Moffatt, Rosse & Joffey, 1967; Dicke, van Noord & van Bekkum, 1973; Barr & Whang-Peng, 1975; Everett &
Perkins, 1976; van Bekkum, 1977; van der Engh et al., 1980; Goldschneider et al., 1980). Furthermore, unrecognizable haemopoietic precursor cells increase in size the closer they approach the stage of recognizable blast cells (Metcalf & MacDonald, 1975; van Bekkum et al., 1979; Goldschneider et al., 1980; Bol & Williams, 1980).
In human bone marrow, few cells fulfill the morphological criteria established for PSC, so that a continuous supply appears possible only under two alternative hypotheses: (i) these cells cycle at rates much higher than those of recognizable bone-marrow cells (Lajtha, 1975); or (ii) since PSC are known to migrate between the various sites of regenerating haemopoiesis via the blood stream, a considerable fraction may reside in other tissues such as the spleen or even the brain (Bartlett, 1982), and continuously immigrate into the bone marrow. Here, they do not necessarily pass through a larger number of cell cycles but start growing into blast cells. This latter hypothesis seemed worth investigating, although experiments have been reported suggesting a limited potential of circulating PSC under steady-state conditions (Micklem, Anderson & Ross, 1975).
Both these hypotheses have been tested in the human bone marrow by quantitative \(^{14}\mathrm{C}\)-autoradiography. This technique allows rates of synthesis to be determined in individual, morphologically identified cells. In this study, all cells not allocatable to the erythroid and myeloid series cells and exhibiting some resemblance to lymphoid cells (except for the small and mature lymphocytes), have been included in the category of lymphoid cells. We therefore presume that the PSC and some of their progeny are contained in this morphological compartment. If the second hypothesis were true, only a few lymphoid cells could be expected to synthesize DNA. However, these should synthesize proteins at a high rate in order to support the considerable changes in structure associated with the growth from rather small cells into large blasts.
## MATERIAL AND METHODS
Bone marrow was aspirated from two healthy adult volunteers. In one case it was taken from the sternum and in the other from the iliac crest. The volunteers had agreed to a powerful aspiration. Thereby we intended to include in the samples a representative number of marginal bone-marrow cells, which appeared advisable in view of the finding of an unequal spatial distribution of the PSC in the bone marrow (Lord & Hendry, 1972; Lord, Testa & Hendry, 1975). One part of the samples was used for preparing Pappenheim smears. The other part was immediately suspended in TC medium 199 and passed through a glass pore filter in order to obtain a single cell suspension. The suspension was split into two parts, one of which was prepared for incubation with \(\left[^{13}\mathrm{H}\right]\)- and \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\)-thymidine (TdR), according to the method designed for cell kinetic studies in humans and described in detail elsewhere (Dörmer, 1973; 1981; Dörmer et al., 1975). In brief, \(5 \times 10^{-5}\) M 5-fluoro-2-deoxyuridine was added to block the pathway of endogenous synthesis of TdR monophosphate. \(10^{-3}\) M deoxycytidine was added to prevent a depletion of the deoxycytidine triphosphate pool following the addition of \(10^{-5}\) M \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\) TdR. With this latter compound (specific activity \(56\ \mathrm{mCi/mmol}\)) the suspension was incubated in a shaking water bath at \(37.2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3 and 7 min, respectively. Under the above conditions of incubation, the TdR incorporation rate between the 3rd and 7th min of incubation with TdR equals the DNA synthesis rate. This was evaluated autoradiographically as described below. Knowing the rate of TdR incorporation into the DNA of individual cells and the amount of thymine to be duplicated during one complete S phase (6.3 \(\times 10^{-15}\) mol for human diploid cells), the DNA synthesis time per cell compartment could be calculated.
The other part of the suspension was used for incubation with \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\)-labelled amino acids. In one case \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\)-l-leucine (final specific activity \(28.4\ \mathrm{mCi/mmol}\)), and in the other \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\)-l-lysine (final specific activity \(14.0\ \mathrm{mCi/mmol}\)) were added. In the leucine experiment, the incubation times varied between 2 and \(40\ \mathrm{min}\), whereas only one incubation time of 15 min was chosen in the case of lysine. The procedure of incubation was much the same as that for \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\mathrm{TdR}\), except for omitting the antimetabolite 5-fluorodeoxyuridine and deoxycytidine in the amino-acid experiments.
Following incubation, smears were prepared on microscopic slides, fixed in absolute methanol for \(48\ \mathrm{hr}\) and provided with \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\)-standard sources. The autoradiographs were processed using Kodak AR.10 stripping film and exposure times were chosen, in the leucine experiment, ranging between 8 and 150 days in order to obtain roughly equal grain densities over the cells for all incubation times. After Giemsa staining the grain densities over the standards and labelled cells were evaluated by an automated grain counter and converted into mol of labelled precursor from the known radioactivity of the standard sources and the final specific activities of the labelled compounds in the incubation flasks. Under the conditions chosen for \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\mathrm{TdR}\) labelling, the \(\left[^{14}\mathrm{C}\right]\mathrm{TdR}\) incorporation rate equals the DNA synthesis rate. In the case of amino-acid incorporation, it had to be shown first that there was no significant intracellular pool of cold compounds mixing with the exogenously labelled analogue and leading to an underestimation of the true amino-acid incorporation rate. At least thirty cells per incubation time and cell compartment were measured in the amino-acid experiments, and 50-100 in the TdR experiments.
Each labelled cell located at random on the slides during evaluation, was assigned to one of the classical morphological cell compartments as described by Wintrobe (1981), and its grain density measured. Those cells exhibiting some similarity to the lymphatic series, but not fulfilling the morphological criteria of either small mature lymphocytes or recognizable erythroid or myeloid cells, were classified as lymphoid cells. There was a minor fraction of lymphoid cells showing some morphological criteria of the monocytic series, as described by Everett & Perkins (1976). These were also included in the category of lymphoid cells. The majority of cells in this category resembled rat cells, identified by Goldschneider et al. (1980) as PSC, using the fluorescence-activated cell sorter, and illustrated in their paper by colour prints. Since the true frequency of bone-marrow cells may be altered in the autoradiographs due to the filtration and incubation procedure, all frequency determinations were performed on the Pappenheim smears immediately prepared after bone-marrow puncture.
Rib bone marrow from two haematologically normal patients undergoing renal surgery was investigated morphologically. The ribs were cut into small pieces. Subsequently, the marrow was carefully removed from the bone pieces by repeated flushing with TC medium 199 using syringes and needles of different size. After removal of bone fragments and fat from the suspension, the cells were concentrated by centrifugation. Finally, one droplet of fetal calf serum was added to the cells and smears were prepared and Pappenheim stained. The frequency of lymphoid cells was determined by scoring 5000 nucleated bone-marrow cells per case.
## RESULTS
During the initial 15 min of incubation of human bone-marrow cells with l-leucine, incorporation rose in a linear fashion. This is illustrated in Fig. 1 for the erythroid series and was comparable in all other cell types, including typical lymphocytes, lymphoid cells and even plasma cells. The deviation from linearity after this initial period is most evident for the early
recognizable cells and can be attributed to inadequate in vitro conditions for longer-lasting incubation periods. However, the initial linear incorporation, starting from point zero, points to a negligible intracellular pool of l-leucine, which implies that the incorporation rate is proportional to the protein synthesis rate. For this reason, the 15 min incorporation of l-lysine in the other experiment is also taken as a measure of the protein synthesis rate. It has been shown by others that there are no fundamental differences in the incorporation of the various essential amino acids (Citoter et al., 1966).
Using \(\left[^{3}\mathbf{H}\right]\) and \(\left[^{14}\mathbf{C}\right]\) TdR, the labelling indices and DNA synthesis times were determined of all proliferative bone-marrow cell compartments including lymphoid cells. By multiplying the frequency of cells of a morphologically defined compartment per 1000 nucleated bone-marrow cells (NBC) by the fraction of labelled cells in that compartment, the relative number of DNA synthesizing cells per compartment was determined. This relative number was divided by the DNA synthesis time in order to derive the birth rate in a compartment per 1000 NBC. A complete analysis in one of the individuals is shown in Table 1. By assuming that all cells of the proliferative compartments have some transition probability according to models similar to that of Smith & Martin (1973), cell-cycle times were calculated for the various morphological compartments by dividing the DNA synthesis times by the fractions of cells in S. The inverse of the cell-cycle time, i.e. the cell-cycle rate, has been plotted in Fig. 2 against the amino-acid incorporation rate. There is a fairly close relationship between both these parameters, implying that haemopoietic cells synthesizing proteins at a high rate also cycle at a correspondingly high rate. Of particular interest are the lymphoid cells, which show a low value for both parameters in both individuals. The highest values were found for proerythroblasts, myeloblasts and promyelocytes.
There was no cell compartment in the bone marrow, except for the plasma cells, exhibiting a rather high protein synthesis rate without a noticeable number of cells in DNA synthesis (Table 1). This compartment, however, is so well defined in terms of morphology and function that plasma cells can be excluded from developing into other bone-marrow cell lineages. We
