further investigated whether there was much heterogeneity of the protein synthesis rate within the lymphoid cells. These cells represent a comparatively small fraction of nucleated bone-marrow cells, making up \(1.96\%\) in one of the normal individuals, and \(1.6\%\) in the other. Also in the rib bone marrow, a comparable frequency of lymphoid cells was determined. It amounted to \(2.1\%\) in one patient and to \(1.66\%\) in the other. Figure 3 indicates that there is only a minor number of lymphoid cells approaching the protein synthesis rate, and thus the growth rate of myeloblasts and proerythroblasts.
## DISCUSSION
The results of Fig. 1 and Table 1 indicate that the protein synthesis rate depends on cell size. This becomes particularly obvious in Table 1 since promyelocytes, although not the most immature cells in the myeloid-cell series, are the largest cells and synthesize proteins at a higher rate than the more immature myeloblasts. An analogous finding has already been communicated by Lindmo (1981), who investigated an exponentially growing cell line, and stated that it is not the stage in the cell cycle but the cell size which determined the protein synthesis rate. The correlation between the rates of protein synthesis and cycling of haemopoietic cells, as demonstrated in Fig. 2, extends this finding and indicates that small cells have long, and large cells short, cell-cycle times.
Concerning the hierarchy of haemopoietic cells, this conclusion suggests a marked increase in the proliferative activity from the PSC to the earliest recognizable blast cells. The peak of proliferative activity at the blast-cell stage is followed by a decrease with advancing maturation. The production rates listed in Table 1 are consistent with a sequential model of haemopoietic cell division from the peak proliferation, i.e. the earliest recognizable blast cells onward (Mackey & Dörmer, 1981). By analysing a large series of data in the literature,
however, it has been shown that this sequential model is inadequate and that a random maturation process with fewer mitotic divisions than previously assumed gives a consistent framework in which to view the available data on human and rodent haemopoiesis (Mackey & Dörmer, 1982).
Irrespective of the correctness of the assumed model of haemopoiesis, the data in Table 1 show that the birth rate in the ascending limb of haemopoietic cell proliferation, i.e. in the lymphoid cells, is too low to account for a continuous supply of the rapidly proliferating, early recognizable human blast-cell compartments on the one hand, and for a continuous self-renewal on the other. Comparable conditions were observed in case 2 (not shown in Table 1). Furthermore, the number of lymphoid cells (Fig. 3) approaching the protein synthesis rate of proerythoblasts and myeloblasts is also far too low to be consistent with the assumption of a steady supply of recognizable blast cells by growth without significant numbers of division. The properties of lymphoid cells in the human bone marrow do not support either of the two hypotheses raised, since (i) their proliferative activity and birth rate is too low for self-maintenance of the compartment and a continuous supply of differentiating cells; and (ii) rapid morphological changes are unlikely due to the rather low protein synthesis rate.
It has been emphasized by Lord and co-workers (1972, 1975) and Frassoni, Testa & Lord (1982) that PSC, as well as committed precursor cells, exhibit a distinct spatial distribution in the bone marrow. The highest frequency of PSC associated with the highest suicide rate was found for cells obtained from areas adjacent to the bone (marginal PSC). We have tried to include these marginal PSC in our samples by aspirating the bone marrow with force. The finding of a comparable frequency of lymphoid cells in the bone marrow prepared from the ribs indicates that we have in fact investigated representative samples.
Since all cells encountered in the bone-marrow smears have been attributed to some morphological subunit, the committed haemopoietic precursor cells most likely have been ranked with the recognizable early compartments such as myeloblasts and proerythroblasts. This would be in line with the high proliferative activity of these cell compartments (Mackey & Dörmer, 1982), which seems to be much the same in the committed precursor compartments, according to the available data (Moore, Williams & Metcalf, 1973; van Bekkum et al., 1979; Wagemaker & Visser, 1981). The present results indicate that there is no complete maintenance of committed haemopoietic precursor cells from the pluripotent level under steady-state conditions in man, provided that human PSC show comparable morphology to that of other species, such as primates. This view is further supported by in vitro data showing that precursor cells committed to the granulocyte-macrophage lineage can be induced to self-replicate in culture for long periods of time (Dexter & Lajtha, 1982). A functional sub-compartment is therefore assumed to exist within the recognizable early bone-marrow cell compartments, with a fraction of cells distinguished by some in vivo capacity of self-maintenance.
